You are fixing a bug in the Go repository checked out at /echo (this is the labstack/echo v4 web framework). Do not use the internet. Do not inspect any other copy/version of echo on the machine, and do NOT use git history (`git log`/`git show`/comparing other commits) — work only from the current file contents in /echo.

BUG REPORT (from the user): There is a bug in /echo/bind.go. Once fixed, these tests should pass (they are not present in the repository; their names describe the expected behaviour):
  - `TestDefaultBinder_bindDataToMap`
  - `TestDefaultBinder_bindDataToMap/ok,_bind_to_map[string][]string`

ROOT CAUSE: in `func (b *DefaultBinder) bindData(destination interface{}, data map[string][]string, tag string) error` (around line 120 of /echo/bind.go) the map branch is:
```go
	// Map
	if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map {
		for k, v := range data {
			val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))
		}
		return nil
	}
```
It always stores only the FIRST value of each `[]string` as a `string`. For a destination of type `map[string][]string` that panics (`reflect.Set: value of type string is not assignable to type []string`), and for other map value types it silently does the wrong thing.

REQUIRED FIX (minimal, in /echo/bind.go only) — support the limited map destinations:
```go
	// Support binding to limited Map destinations:
	// - map[string][]string,
	// - map[string]string <-- (binds first value from data slice)
	// - map[string]interface{}
	// You are better off binding to struct as this package does not support binding to arbitrary map types.
	if typ.Kind() == reflect.Map {
		switch typ.Elem().Kind() {
		case reflect.Slice:
			for k, v := range data {
				val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k).Convert(typ.Key()), reflect.ValueOf(v))
			}
		case reflect.String:
			for k, v := range data {
				val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k).Convert(typ.Key()), reflect.ValueOf(v[0]))
			}
		case reflect.Interface:
			for k, v := range data {
				val.SetMapIndex(reflect.ValueOf(k).Convert(typ.Key()), reflect.ValueOf(v))
			}
		default:
			return errors.New("unsupported map type")
		}
		return nil
	}
```
Additionally, guard against a nil destination map (a nil map panics on SetMapIndex): before the switch, add
```go
		if val.IsNil() {
			val.Set(reflect.MakeMap(typ))
		}
```
so that binding into a nil map (e.g. `new(map[string]string)`) works instead of panicking. Keep `errors` imported (it already is). Do not reformat unrelated code, do not add features, do not change the struct-binding path.

BEFORE EDITING: read /echo/bind_test.go and /echo/binder_test.go to see if any pre-existing test exercises the map branch, and make sure your change keeps those green.

